well the holidays should be a time to  let loose with your family and your  friends but for those battling substance  abuse it can really be a trigger for  some bad habits well john langler looks  at how families can work together to  spot trouble science  a study by one poll shows the average  american drinks double the amount of  alcohol over the holidays than any other  time of the year it can present a  challenge especially for those early in  their sobriety  considering the amount of alcohol at  holiday parties those in early sobriety  may feel uncomfortable in social  settings but addiction specialist sarah  o'brien says there are things you can do  to protect yourself or help a loved one  who is newly sober  have a support system readily available  you know through the phone whether it's  text message or a phone call someone  that's sober in recovery that you can  reach out to if things you know get  stressful o'brien also says have an exit  plan in case things become overwhelming  she suggests holding a non-alcoholic  beverage most of the time people aren't  going to ask what you want to sip on or  be tempted to give you a cocktail if  something is already in your hand and  don't feel obligated to attend an event  you know if you feel as though you can't  you know attend these parties i think  that's okay i think you know going to a  recovery based meeting or going to a  sober event you know is just as  important if not you know a lot safer  family and friends can also help by  hosting an entirely sober event or  assisting in diverting conversations to  something else and watching for clues  that someone might be struggling with  their sobriety like a change in behavior  you know from maybe a happy-go-lucky  on-time individual to someone who's  maybe not showing up for events or as  late  disheveled appearance  you know  maybe a little bit lashing out a little  bit more a little bit more irritable  discontent always remember your mental  health and your safety and sobriety is  most important if you're struggling with  addiction or mental health at any time  we have resources available at eight  newsnow dot com john langler live local  now
 

